{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 200,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2880,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get started, we \nwanted to let you know we have special,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6080,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limited edition new merch in honor of the 50th  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8800,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anniversary of Space Shuttle patent. \nMore info at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter has eluded us for many decades. \nEven our most advanced particle colliders  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sophisticated underground detectors \nhave come up short. But it may be that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can finally solve this mystery \nwith a much simpler experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28000,
    "dDurationMs": 11080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "involving a ray of light, a \ngood clock, and the planet Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over 80% of the mass in the universe is in the \nform of some mysterious, invisible, unseeable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44640,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "substance that, for want of any idea whatsoever \nwhat it is, we call dark matter. At first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many of us thought that it was just some sort \nof regular stuff that wasn’t easy to see—maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of small, dense, non-shining bodies like \nbrown dwarfs or neutron stars or even black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61600,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. But as we ruled out most of the possible \nmass ranges for these, more and more of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter hunting has been about finding some \nkind of exotic particle—Neutrinos, axions, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, again, after years and years of \nlooking for the dark matter particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re in the same place with this \nhypothesis—there’s just no credible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence in favor of any hypothesis \nfor what dark matter really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like when you look for your keys in the usual \nspots, don’t find them so check under the couch,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the cat, inside the refrigerator. \nBut when you still don’t find them you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double check your pockets. Maybe it was \nin one of the obvious places all along."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are indeed a couple of pockets still to \ncheck, where “pockets” means range of masses for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112240,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compact bodies. The most promising under-explored \nrange is 10^17 to 10^23 g, roughly the mass range  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of asteroids. Now, dark matter can’t be asteroids \nbecause there’s no conceivable way to make 5 times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128480,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass in asteroids than there is in stars—after \nall, asteroid material is made inside stars. There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren’t that many stars, and also dark matter \nhad to be around before the first stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is an option for a truly invisible \nobject in that mass range, and one that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been around since near the beginning \nand that's the primordial black hole (PBH).  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days, black holes are formed in the \ndeaths of massive stars and their masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start at around 3 times the Sun’s mass. But, \nas we’ve seen before on this show, there is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way to produce asteroid-mass black holes, and \nthat’s right after the Big Bang. Back then,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there were tiny variations in the density of the \nhyper-dense sea of matter and energy that filled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe. Some of these would later collapse \nunder gravity into galaxies and galaxy clusters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176920,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but right at the beginning the most dense such \nregions could have collapsed directly into black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. These primordial black holes could have a \nrange of masses depending on yet-unknown details  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the very early universe. But if we \ntune our Big Bang parameters just right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can get enough PHBs in the right mass \nrange to account for all of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not only have we talked about PBHs before, \nwe’ve talked about PBHs as dark matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AND we’ve talked about the possibility of \ndetecting PBHs from the scars they might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leave as they pass through the Earth, or even the \nMoon. But such a detection is going to take quite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit of luck—the Earth and Moon make pretty \nsmall targets on the scale of the solar system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and distinguishing the scars from old \nPBH impacts isn’t necessarily so easy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226040,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that we may not even \nneed PBHs to hit planets in order to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231600,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect them. Recent studies have shown \nthat our entire solar system may serve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a gigantic PBH detector, with these \nlittle black holes leaving their faint  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241320,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but very possibly detectable gravitational \nsignature on the orbits of the planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247360,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s run some numbers to see \nif this can really make this "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work. And by “we” I mean the scientists who \nare actively figuring this out as we speak.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One paper I’ll focus on in particular Tung \nTran, Sarah Geller, Benjamin Lehmann and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Kaiser and it was published just this \nSeptember and I think does the best job at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267720,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assessing the plausibility of turning the \nsolar system into a black hole detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with dark matter. Based on the orbits \nof stars in the Milky Way, we have a pretty good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea of the distribution of dark matter in our \ngalaxy. The density of dark matter at our solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282920,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system’s position in the galaxy is about 7x10^-25 \ng/cm^-3 or about the equivalent of the mass of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proton in every sugar cube of volume. So, if dark \nmatter is a proton-mass particle, something like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 million of them pass through you every second. \nExperiments trying to detect dark matter particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assume that they stream through our detectors in \nenormous numbers, so that even if there’s a very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low probability of interaction, we’ll eventually \ncatch some. Although apparently not yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316080,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more massive the dark matter particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lower the number density. If dark matter \nis a compact object like a black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then they’d be pretty spread out. For example, if \nall dark matter consists of Sun-mass black holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’d need to be one per 15 cubic \nlight year volume to do the job."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what mass range might these PBHs have if \nthey really do account for all of dark matter?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341040,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astronomer’s favourite way to constrain has been \nwith gravitational lensing. Although black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are invisible, their gravitational fields warp \nthe paths of light rays passing close by. If a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole passes between us an a more distant \nstar, that star will briefly brighten as its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light gets focused towards us in an effect called \nmicrolensing. The OGLE survey recently published  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new results from their decades-long effort to \ncount compact objects in the Magellanic clouds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this way. They rule out huge mass ranges \nof primordial black holes as dark matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything from half the mass of the moon up \nto about 1% of the mass of the sun, roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384080,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the boundary between brown dwarfs and proper \nstars- that’s a factor of a million in mass. \n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390120,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in fact the only remaining mass window that’s \nstill wide open to potentially account for all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396480,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dark matter is the so-called “asteroid mass \nrange”, from around 10^17 grams to 10^23 grams, or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404240,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less than 0.1% of the moon's mass. If such small \nblack holes make up all dark matter we’re going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411680,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to need a lot of them. If PBHs are at the bottom \nof range—10^17 grams—then we’d expect there to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420320,
    "dDurationMs": 5187,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one somewhere in the inner solar system right now. \nAt the upper end of this range of asteroid-mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425507,
    "dDurationMs": 6493,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes, we might expect one to pass through \nthe solar system every century. In between these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremes we have them passing through the solar \nsystem on timescales of months to decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that is frequent enough that we should \nthink about how to detect one when it comes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443320,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re not going to see a primordial black hole \nwith a telescope. In the asteroid mass range,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449760,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our PBHs have event horizons that range from the \nsubatomic to the microscopic. So even if they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible they wouldn’t be visible. Instead, we’d \nspot a PBH passing through our solar system in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same way we discovered dark matter in the \nfirst place—from its gravitational influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When an interstellar object enters \nthe solar system it almost always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475120,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exits again—the speed it gathers falling \ninto the Sun’s gravitational field is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by definition, exactly what it needs \nto escape. Add to this the fact that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an interstellar object is on a different \norbit to the solar system, so it’s already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming in hot—with some appreciable fraction \nof the galactic orbital speed of 220 km/s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is that a black hole fly-through \nis going to be pretty quick, with a passage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the inner solar system lasting a few \nmonths at most. If this is the sort of black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole produced by the death of a star—a stellar \nblack hole—then its effect on the planetary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system would be devastating. Orbits may \nbe significantly altered, planets ejected,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515560,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the black hole may even capture \nplanets into its own orbit. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the effect of an asteroid-mass primordial black \nhole would be no more significant than an actual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstellar asteroid passing through the \nsolar system—which means almost no effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets are many millions to trillions \nof times more massive than any such PBH,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the acceleration a planet experiences \ndue to that passage will be commensurately tiny.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543240,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But tiny isn’t zero. Think about it this way: \nimagine you and another car are side by side on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550640,
    "dDurationMs": 10160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the highway both going exactly 100.00 km/hr. Your \ncruise control glitches and you speed up to 100.01  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "km/hr. You don't notice that right away, but \nan hour later your car will be 10 meters ahead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s an entire car length, which you’ll \ndefinitely notice. In the same way, a tiny,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts-per-trillion change in a planet’s \nposition will be visible over years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say a PBH in the upper-middlish of \nour asteroid-mass range—10^21 grams—passes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586080,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through our solar system a little way outside \nthe orbit of Mars. The red planet’s change in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed would be pretty much unmeasurable at \nthe time. But after a decade it would add  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to a difference in position of about \n1m compared to the expected location.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That also sounds pretty hard to measure, \ngiven the 10s of millions of km distance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607560,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between Earth and Mars. But there are two \nfactors that make this actually possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first is that the motion of the planets \nis predictable. With clockwork precision,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620320,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planets are slaves to the law of \ngravity—whether Newton’s or Einstein’s.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626040,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we know the positions and velocities of a \nsystem of gravitating bodies at one point in time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can trace their motion far into the \nfuture. Neptune was discovered based on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635680,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small discrepancy in the motion of Uranus, and \nthat was calculated with pen and paper back in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the middle 1800s. Modern computers can \ncompute the future motion of the planets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646560,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with incredible precision, although including \nthe gravitational influence of the many smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "residents of the solar system starts to make \nit complicated, and I’ll come back to that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657040,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Knowing precisely where a planet is, even down to \nthe centimeter, isn’t helpful if we can’t actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664800,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure the distance to the planet with that same \nprecision. But fortunately, that’s also within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humanity’s skill set. We don’t measure distances \nto planets directly. Instead, we measure the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel time of light. Conveniently, the speed of \nlight in the near vacuum of interplanetary space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682320,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is constant and extremely well known—so timing \nits round trip from Earth and back gives us a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690480,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance that’s as precise as the stopwatch \nwe use to time that trip. And we do have some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exceedingly precise atomic stopwatches these days.\nOne of the most precisely measured distances in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solar system is the Earth-Moon \nseparation. It’s known to about 1 mm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "precision thanks to the retroreflectors—fancy \nmirrors—placed on the lunar surface by the US,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Soviets, and now India. The precision of \nour measurement of the Earth-Moon distance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes the moon sound like a good option for a good \nblack hole detector. But it’s actually not our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "best option. The fact that the moon is so close \nmeans we can't really treat it as a single point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731120,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Earth and Moon are big, slightly irregular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balls that squish and stretch in each \nothers gravity. Although we can measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738720,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the distance to those retroreflectors with amazing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "precision it's hard to convert that to \na well defined distance to the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The trick then is to look farther, to Mars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749640,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While Mars doesn’t yet have a retroreflector \nbig enough this, we have something that may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be even better--our own satellites orbiting \nMars. At any one time over the past 20 years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have had at least three different functioning \nspacecraft in Martian orbit. It's straightforward  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to get an exact distance to any one of \nthese based on the signal travel time, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "satellites can use complex triangulation to get \ntheir own altitude above the Martian surface.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By collecting these distances over time and for \nmultiple spacecraft, we can now define and keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "track of a robust \"distance to Mars\", and have \nbeen able to do so for 20 years. We can likely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790080,
    "dDurationMs": 9400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spot the 1 meter offset caused by the 10^21g PBH \nwe saw earlier, and perhaps even the 10cm-ish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799480,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offset caused by a PBH 10 times smaller. That \ncovers half of the possible range for PBH masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808280,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so apparently we can both predict \nand measure the location of Mars with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812040,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the precision needed to tell if it \nstrayed by a foot or two from where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was supposed to be. How do we know \nthat was caused by a passing black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solar system is a busy place, packed \nwith planets, moons, dwarf planets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroids, and comets. The mini-tweaks that \nthe Earth and Mars might get from a PBH is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832320,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of millions, all of which must be accounted \nfor if the signal from the black hole is to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "separated from the noise. In fact, any asteroid \nin the inner solar system with a mass comparable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to our PBH would need to be included in \ncalculations, otherwise its gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effects will completely wash out the effect we’re \nsearching for. The good news is that, while 10^20  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grams or so makes a black hole as small as \nan atom, it makes an asteroid  of that mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864600,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about a 100 km across, and all asteroids \nthat big are all pretty well tracked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, a PBH flyby will have a very different \ntrajectory to a solar system asteroid. Objects  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877840,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the solar system orbit pretty close to \nthe same plane that the planets orbit in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882680,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all at speeds low enough to keep \nthem bound in the Sun’s gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. A passing PBH will be moving quite a bit \nfaster, and could come in at any angle relative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the plane of the solar system. In-plane \nflybys will tend to tweak a planet’s speed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but keep it in the same plane. Steeper flybys will \ntend to tug a planet slightly out of that plane,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905600,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "altering its orbital inclination. Because of \nthis, the change in a planet’s position due  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911240,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to an extrasolar object versus a solar \nsystem object may be quite distinct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s talk about the two experiments \nwe can do. One we can do right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922080,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and one is for the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924400,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We currently have around 20 years of \nprecision data on the position of Mars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928760,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in principle we could find the signs of past \nPBH passes in these data. Some preliminary efforts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936360,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been made to analyze this, but not with \nconclusive results because it’s going to require  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some very sophisticated simulations to analyze \nthe data properly. The proper way to do this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947680,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is by running many, many computer simulations \nto map the space of possible ways in which the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit of Mars could evolve in response to the \ngravitational influence of known and unknown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar system objects. That will tell us \nwhether, at some time in the past 30 years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964680,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars strayed from this space of probable orbits \nin a way consistent with an interaction with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a massive flyby. Then we add potential \nextrasolar visitors to our simulation and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977760,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see how well Mars’ trajectory is explained \nby different flyby masses and trajectories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no direct way to tell if such a \nflyby was really a PBH or just an extra-solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989480,
    "dDurationMs": 8760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroid of the same mass. If we find it in \nthis old data then by now it’ll be long gone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we can’t look for it with our telescopes. We do \nknow that there are such asteroids living between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004200,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stars—for example, Oumuamua, although it’s \nway below our desired mass range. However, if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011440,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter is made of primordial black holes at \nthese masses then those PBHs need to be way, way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019280,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more abundant that extra-solar asteroids. So if we \nfind way too many such kicks to the Martian orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028480,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in those 20 years of data, it’s good evidence \nthat they’re coming from PHBs and not asteroids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so that’s the experiment we can do now, \nand astronomers are indeed working on this. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041440,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we’d really want is to get more direct \nevidence. Monitoring future data from Mars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can allow us to notice a change in its \nmotion within a few years if we’re lucky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then it might be possible to track the \noffending source of gravity as it coasts away from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060640,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solar system. If it’s an asteroid then it’ll \nbe visible to a sufficiently giant telescope. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065560,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s a black hole it will not be. So maybe we’ll \ndiscover what dark matter is by observing nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071760,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be that to find the next level of \naction in particle physics we'll need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build a particle collider the size of the solar \nsystem. Well, we already have a solar system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081480,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sized particle detector that could discover \ndark matter. Assuming dark matter is made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086160,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of countless subatomic-sized black holes with \nthe masses of asteroids. In which case we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091400,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solve dark matter from miniscule shifts they \ncause in planetary paths through spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting PBS. \nScientists often find themselves in some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102960,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most remote corners of the world. And whether \nyou’re in a remote location or just working from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "home, the need for reliable, secure communication \nand data transfer remains important. With Anydesk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "file transfer functionality allows for \nfast, secure, and direct file transfers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119040,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bypassing the cloud entirely. With coverage all \nover the world Anydesk remains fast and reliable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with over 99% uptime. And when you’re \nworking on that groundbreaking research,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t want any distractions. Thankfully \nAnydesk features a clean user interface and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple user experience as well as compatibility \nacross multiple computing and mobile platforms.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141160,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even if your astronomical data set is still \nrunning on WindowsXP9, Anydesk still has you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "covered with backwards compatibility. Anydesk is \navailable for personal use, but if your research  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires a lot of global coordination, Anydesk \noffers custom-tailored business plans. To learn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159840,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more about Anydesk, go to anydesk.com/spacetime \nor check out the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165880,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On February 18th, 1975, a patent for the future \nwas born. Patent US3866863A became the blueprint  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174440,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for NASA’s Space Shuttle—the first reusable \nspacecraft. Over 30 years and 135 missions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it shaped modern spaceflight. Now, 50 years \nlater, we’re celebrating this legacy with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188720,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "special edition PBS Space Time merch. \nFeaturing the original patent blueprint,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192680,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re launching the first Space Time \nHardcover Notebook in black, white, blue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and red—perfect for tracking your 2025 ideas, \ngoals, or even your own future patents. We’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also re-releasing the Space Time multifunctional \npen in black, white, silver, red, and blue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1209240,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus a special edition hoodie and t-shirt in \nmultiple colors with the patent number on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sleeve. And by popular demand, all Space Time \nt-shirts—including past designs—will now be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220080,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "available in a female cut. Check it all out at \npbsspacetime.com/shop—link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
